examples.question_text:
Estimate this area using one application of the trapezoidal rule. Draw the straight boundary used for the estimate and explain whether the estimate is less than or greater than the actual area.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[thick] (0,2.4)--(0,0)--(3,0)--(3,3.6);
\draw[thick] (0,2.4) .. controls (0.8,3.3) and (2,3.7) .. (3,3.6);
\path (0,1.2) node[left] {$24\text{ m}$} (3,1.8) node[right] {$36\text{ m}$} (1.5,0) node[below] {$20\text{ m}$};
\draw (0,0.2)--(0.2,0.2)--(0.2,0);
\draw (2.8,0)--(2.8,0.2)--(3,0.2);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
$\begin{align*} A &\approx \frac{h}{2}(d_f+d_l)\\ &\approx \frac{20}{2}(24+36)\\ &\approx 600\text{ m}^2 \end{align*}$

Join the ends of the two parallel sides to form the approximating trapezium.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{estimate}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[thick] (0,2.4)--(0,0)--(3,0)--(3,3.6);
\draw[thick] (0,2.4) .. controls (0.8,3.3) and (2,3.7) .. (3,3.6);
\draw[thick,dashed,estimate] (0,2.4)--(3,3.6);
\path (0,1.2) node[left] {$24\text{ m}$} (3,1.8) node[right] {$36\text{ m}$} (1.5,0) node[below] {$20\text{ m}$};
\draw (0,0.2)--(0.2,0.2)--(0.2,0);
\draw (2.8,0)--(2.8,0.2)--(3,0.2);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The dashed straight boundary lies below the curve, leaving out area. The estimate is less than the actual area.

examples.question_text:
The middle offset is halfway along the $27\text{ m}$ baseline.

a. Estimate the area using one application of the trapezoidal rule.
b. Estimate the area using two applications of the trapezoidal rule.
Give both estimates to the nearest square metre.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[thick] (0,2.08)--(0,0)--(4,0)--(4,2.92);
\draw[thick] (2,0)--(2,2.72);
\draw[thick] (0,2.08) .. controls (0.7,2.4) and (1.3,2.6) .. (2,2.72) .. controls (2.7,2.84) and (3.3,2.92) .. (4,2.92);
\path (0,1.04) node[left] {$20.8\text{ m}$} (2,1.36) node[left] {$27.2\text{ m}$} (4,1.46) node[right] {$29.2\text{ m}$};
\draw (0,0.2)--(0.2,0.2)--(0.2,0);
\draw (2,0.2)--(2.2,0.2)--(2.2,0);
\draw (3.8,0)--(3.8,0.2)--(4,0.2);
\draw[thin] (0,-0.35)--(0,-0.65) (4,-0.35)--(4,-0.65);
\draw[<->] (0,-0.5)--(4,-0.5);
\path (2,-0.5) node[below] {$27\text{ m}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Estimate the total area using two applications of the trapezoidal rule. For each strip, explain whether its estimate is greater than or less than its actual area.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[thick] (0,3)--(0,0)--(4,0)--(4,2.8);
\draw[thick] (2,0)--(2,1.8);
\draw[thick] (0,3) .. controls (0.65,2) and (1.35,1.8) .. (2,1.8) .. controls (2.65,2.55) and (3.35,2.8) .. (4,2.8);
\path (0,1.5) node[left] {$30\text{ m}$} (2,0.9) node[left] {$18\text{ m}$} (4,1.4) node[right] {$28\text{ m}$} (1,0) node[below] {$14\text{ m}$} (3,0) node[below] {$14\text{ m}$};
\draw (0,0.2)--(0.2,0.2)--(0.2,0);
\draw (2,0.2)--(2.2,0.2)--(2.2,0);
\draw (3.8,0)--(3.8,0.2)--(4,0.2);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
$\begin{align*} A &\approx A_1+A_2\\ &\approx \frac{14}{2}(30+18)+\frac{14}{2}(18+28)\\ &\approx 336+322\\ &\approx 658\text{ m}^2 \end{align*}$

The dashed lines show the straight boundaries used for the two trapeziums.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{estimate}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[thick] (0,3)--(0,0)--(4,0)--(4,2.8);
\draw[thick] (2,0)--(2,1.8);
\draw[thick] (0,3) .. controls (0.65,2) and (1.35,1.8) .. (2,1.8) .. controls (2.65,2.55) and (3.35,2.8) .. (4,2.8);
\draw[thick,dashed,estimate] (0,3)--(2,1.8)--(4,2.8);
\path (1,0) node[below] {Strip 1} (3,0) node[below] {Strip 2};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Strip 1: the straight boundary lies above the curve, adding area, so the estimate is greater than the actual area.

Strip 2: the straight boundary lies below the curve, leaving out area, so the estimate is less than the actual area.
